A build system: the decisions of make as a relation
===================================================

Make executes, Sprite computes.  This example builds a toy C project: four
sources, two headers, a static library of two objects, and a program.  The
Curry module Build states what make knows as a relation between names: the
pattern rules, what a target reads, what is stale, and in which order the
stale targets go.  From that one relation it answers every question of a
build: the plan, the targets that can run now, the targets a change touches,
and the targets that two rules claim.  The Python driver go.py owns the
world: it writes the files, scans the includes, reads the stamps, runs the
recipes in a thread pool, and prints the status.  Curry never touches the
file system.  Sprite itself builds with configure and GNU make; this example
does not replace them, and its rules are not Sprite's.


1. What the example shows about functional logic programming
-------------------------------------------------------------

Each question of a build is one function of Build.curry.  Each function
states a relation and a policy.

Which rule builds a target?  Each equation of rulesFor is one rule, and
every equation that matches is one answer:

    rulesFor _ (stem ++ ".o") =
      (stem ++ ".o", [stem ++ ".c"], ["cc", "-c", "-o", stem ++ ".o", stem ++ ".c"])
    rulesFor p t | t == lib = (lib, objs, "ar" : "rcs" : lib : objs)
      where (lib, objs) = library p
    rulesFor p t | t == prog = (prog, objs ++ [lib], "cc" : "-o" : prog : objs ++ [lib])
      where ...
    rulesFor p t | (t, ins, words) =:= anyOf (explicit p) = (t, ins, words)
      where ins, words free

The first equation is the pattern rule %.o: %.c of make.  Its functional
pattern stem ++ ".o" matches every object name and binds the stem, so the
rule names its source and its recipe from the stem.  The second and the
third name their targets.  The last takes the explicit rules of the project,
rules written for one target each: the free variables ins and words unify
with the inputs and the recipe of every entry for the target, and each entry
is one answer.  An explicit rule for a new name adds a target; one for a
target of the project adds a second rule.  So "which rule builds this
target" is a non-deterministic question.  The policy for a well-formed
target is that the question has one answer.  The set function rulesOf
collects the answers as a list,

    rulesOf p t = sortValues (set2 rulesFor p t)

and the rest of the module reads the rules through that list: no rule means
a source or a header, one rule is the normal case, and two rules are the
ambiguity below.

What does a target read?  The relation inputs gives the direct inputs of a
target: the inputs of its rule, each with the headers it includes, directly
or through another header.  The includes come from a table that Python
scanned.  A header that includes a header is followed through the table,
and a list of the files seen stops a cycle of includes, as an include guard
does.  A file that no rule builds reads nothing.

What is stale?  The relation stale is over a table of stamps that Python
supplies, a list of (file, stamp) pairs with one integer per file that
exists.  A target is stale when it is missing, when it is older than an
input, or when an input is stale:

    stale p table = staleFrom p table []

    staleFrom p table seen t
      | t `elem` seen = False
      | otherwise     = case lookup t table of
          Nothing -> True
          Just s  -> any (\i -> staleFrom p table (t : seen) i
                                  || maybe False (s <) (lookup i table))
                         (inputs p t)

A missing file has no entry, so a source that is not there makes its object
stale forever.  Curry compares stamps and nothing else.  The list seen holds
the targets on the path from the question to this file: a target on its own
path reads nothing more, so a cycle of rules does not send the question
round for ever.

What to build, and in which order?  The plan is the stale targets in
dependency order: waves lays them out in layers, and a layer holds the stale
targets whose stale inputs are all in earlier layers.  The policy is a
topological order over the relation.  A cycle leaves targets that never
enter a layer; the guard of layers fails then, and the plan has no value.  A
cycle is a failure, not an error.  Each entry of the plan carries what the
target reads and the words of its recipe.

What can run now?  The ready set is the stale targets whose inputs are all
up to date:

    ready p table =
      [t | t <- targets p, stale p table t, not (any (stale p table) (inputs p t))]

The driver runs them in parallel, records the new stamps, and asks again.
The scheduler of the build is this one line of Curry.

What does a change touch?  The inverse query affected is the relation of
inputs read backwards.  The target is the unknown, drawn from the targets
with anyOf, and the guard asks whether it reads the file, directly or
through another target:

    affected p f | reads p t f = t
      where t = anyOf (targets p)

Each answer is one value.  The question "what rebuilds if shape.h changes"
needs no second relation and no index from files to targets.  reads carries
the list of the targets on its path, as stale does.

Which targets do two rules claim?  The lint ambiguous lists every target
whose rulesOf has more than one member.  Make has a tie-break rule for that
case, which you have to remember: an explicit rule wins over a pattern rule,
and among pattern rules the shortest stem wins, then the first rule in the
makefile.  Here two rules for one target are two answers to one question,
and the driver reports them and refuses to build.


2. What the example shows about calling Curry from Python
----------------------------------------------------------

The driver go.py owns the world, and the README calls that the boundary.
Python writes the project into a temporary directory, scans each file for
quoted includes with a regular expression, reads the modification time of
each file in nanoseconds as its stamp, runs the recipes, and serves the
status.  Curry gets the project and the table as data and returns names and
rules.  The IO surface of Sprite is small, and a build needs none of it:
every decision is a function of the data Python has in hand.

The project is a Curry record, built with the typed curry.expr:

    curry.expr(Build.Project, sources, LIBRARY, PROGRAM, includes, explicit)

The sources are a list of Python strs, the library and the program are
(name, objects) tuples, the include table is a list of (file, headers)
pairs, and the explicit rules are (target, inputs, words) triples.  A name
is a Python str, which curry.expr converts by the type the constructor
expects, a String, also for a name of one character.  The empty list of
explicit rules converts under [Rule].  Python builds the record once per
project and passes it to every goal.

A deterministic goal has one value, and the driver reads it with

    next(curry.eval(goal, *args, converter='topython'))

The converter turns a list of rules into a list of tuples of lists of str.
The plan is the exception: on a cycle it has no value, so the driver reads
it with next(values, None) and reports the failure.  The inverse query is
non-deterministic, and the driver collects its values with
sorted(curry.eval(...)), because Curry promises no order.

A build lints first: a target that two rules claim stops the build before
the plan.  The build loop then asks Curry for the ready set, submits each
new ready target to a concurrent.futures thread pool, waits for the first
completion, and asks again with the new stamps.  The table it hands to
Curry omits the targets whose recipes run or failed.  A compiler creates
its output before it finishes, and a half-written object with a fresh stamp
would read as up to date; a target that is missing to Curry is stale, and
its dependents are not ready.  Python keeps the running set and the outcome
of each recipe, and nothing of the dependency relation: no order, no count
of pending inputs.  Run go.py with --trace to see each ready set on stderr.
With --jobs 2 the library was ready while report.o and main.o were still
running, because its two objects were done.  The waves in the output are
the static picture of the same relation.

The word cc in a recipe stands for the C compiler of the environment, CC or
cc.  When no compiler and no ar are on the PATH, every recipe runs as a
stand-in that writes a placeholder file in place of the target, so the
example runs on a machine without a compiler and on CI.  The output is the
same in both modes; the stand-in says so on stderr.  The status table and
the plan print no stamps, no times, and no temporary paths, and the driver
sorts what the thread pool returns.

The option --serve PORT serves a status page with http.server from the
standard library.  The page shows the status table, a build button, and a
touch button, and /status.json gives the targets with their inputs and
staleness, the plan, and the ready set as JSON.  Each request calls the same
functions as the tested run.  A richer front end, with rich in the terminal
or nicegui in the browser, would call them too; neither is needed, and the
example does not depend on them.


3. How to run
-------------

Run "make stage" from the root of the repository first.  Then run

    ./run

The run script uses the C++ backend by default.  To pick a backend, set
SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS in the environment:

    SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS=backend:py ./run
    SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS=backend:cxx ./run

To see the ready set after each completion, or to change the width of the
thread pool, pass --trace and --jobs to go.py:

    ../../install/bin/python go.py --trace --jobs 2

To serve the status page, run

    ../../install/bin/python go.py --serve 8000

and open http://127.0.0.1:8000/ in a browser.  Press Ctrl-C to stop it.
The run script does not start the server.


4. Expected output
------------------

The file expected.out holds the exact output of ./run.  The driver prints
the project first, then five steps.  Step 1 is the full build:

    1. full build
    plan: 6 targets
      vec.o     <- vec.c vec.h                cc -c -o vec.o vec.c
      shape.o   <- shape.c shape.h vec.h      cc -c -o shape.o shape.c
      report.o  <- report.c vec.h             cc -c -o report.o report.c
      main.o    <- main.c shape.h vec.h       cc -c -o main.o main.c
      libgeom.a <- vec.o shape.o              ar rcs libgeom.a vec.o shape.o
      demo      <- main.o report.o libgeom.a  cc -o demo main.o report.o libgeom.a
    waves: [vec.o shape.o report.o main.o] [libgeom.a] [demo]
    built: demo libgeom.a main.o report.o shape.o vec.o

Each object reads its source and the headers behind it: shape.o reads vec.h
through shape.h.  The four objects form the first wave, the library the
second, the program the third.  A status table follows, with one line per
target.  Step 2 builds again, and the plan is "nothing to do".  Step 3
touches shape.h:

    3. touch shape.h
    affected: demo libgeom.a main.o shape.o
    ...
    built: demo libgeom.a main.o shape.o
    ...
    rebuilt the affected set: yes

The inverse query predicts the four targets that read shape.h, and the
build then rebuilds exactly those.  vec.o and report.o stay up to date.
Step 4 adds a second rule for main.o, and the lint stops the build:

    4. a second rule for main.o
    explicit:
      main.o <- main.c  cc -c -O2 -o main.o main.c
    ambiguous: main.o has 2 rules
      main.o <- main.c  cc -c -O2 -o main.o main.c
      main.o <- main.c  cc -c -o main.o main.c
    refused: the build does not start

Step 5 adds a rule that closes a cycle: the program writes a header that
its own objects include.  The header is older than the program, so it is
stale, and so is every target that reads it.  No layer can start, and the
plan has no value:

    5. a rule that closes a cycle
    explicit:
      shape.h <- demo  ./demo --emit-header shape.h
    plan: no value (the rules have a cycle)
    status:
      vec.o      up to date
      shape.o    stale
      ...
      shape.h    stale

The output is identical on both backends, and with and without a C
compiler.


5. Limits
---------

Three warm runs of ./run took 0.42 s to 0.46 s on the C++ backend, and two
took 8.3 s on the Python backend.  The Python backend answers a question in
0.1 s to 0.7 s (the ready set on a built tree 0.4 s, the plan 0.7 s, the
stale check of one target 0.2 s), and the run asks dozens of them, so the
questions are nearly all of its time.  A first run, without products under
.curry/, took 1.2 s on the C++ backend and 8.7 s on the Python backend, so
the compile of the module is a small part of the run.  Without a C compiler
the run took 0.35 s on the C++ backend.

A name that a functional pattern produced carries its narrowing, and every
later comparison of that name pays for it again.  A first version of this
module had object as a functional pattern and read the rules from rulesFor
directly.  Its plan after the touch did not finish in 120 s on either
backend.  With a plain object and the rules still read from rulesFor the
plan took 10 s on the C++ backend.  Read through the set function rulesOf,
whose values are plain data, it takes 0.01 s.  So the functional pattern
stays where it carries the meaning, in rulesFor, and the relation reads the
rules as values.

A stamp is an integer that Python computes, here the modification time in
nanoseconds.  A target with the same stamp as an input is up to date, as
in make.  The touch of step 3 waits until the header is newer than every
other file of the project, so a file system with a coarse clock gives the
same output after a pause of one tick.  A content hash is also an integer,
but "older than" has no meaning for it; the relation would then ask whether
the stamp differs from a recorded one.

The waves are the static picture: a layer holds the targets that can run
once the earlier layers are done.  The pool follows the dynamic ready set,
so a target of the second wave may start while the first wave still runs.
A target whose recipe fails stays stale, and its dependents are never
ready; the status table shows them as stale.  A source that is missing
makes its object stale forever in the same way.  The plan sees a cycle
when a target of the cycle is stale, as in step 5; a cycle among targets
that are up to date is nothing to do.  Make reports a cycle before it looks
at a stamp; that check would be one more question, layers over every
target.

The toolchain choice stays out of this example on purpose.  The natural
next rule, a choice of compiler for every target,

    toolchain = "gcc" ? "clang"

with set functions over the targets, is the shape of issue #61: a set
function that starts while a variable is unbound and resumes under
alternatives that narrowed it differently.  The fix in place covers a
choice that is decided before the set function starts; the general case,
the repair at the escape of the capsule, is open.  When it lands, the
example can add the choice and let the lint report a project whose targets
disagree on the toolchain.

The project is a toy by design: a handful of sources, one library, one
program.  The rules of Sprite's own Makefiles are not modelled, and Curry
does not replace GNU make at scale.  A real project would read the sources
of a directory, take the rules from a small file in place of the constants
of go.py, and scan the includes with the compiler's own dependency output.
The relation does not change; only the table that Python supplies grows.
